drafting: geom.UCS
A user coordinate system: an origin, an x axis and a normal,
defining a plane with coordinates of its own.

A geom.UCS is where a geom.Polyline or a
geom.Region lies: its vertices are coordinates in the UCS, and a
solid made from a region is made where its UCS puts it. A region drawn in
the default UCS, the world xy plane, is laid on the face of a part
by giving it a UCS on that face, which is what a CAD program's user
coordinate system is for.

It is a coordinate system, not only a plane: two systems on the same
plane with different origins or x axes place the same outline
differently, and solid.revolve turns about the y axis
through the origin. The y axis is the normal crossed with the
x axis, so the axes are right-handed.

A UCS is made by its constructor, from a normal and points or with the
mouse in a viewer, or by geom.UCS.threepoint from three points.
It is a value and never changes.

See also:
geom.Polyline,
geom.Region,
