The outlines of text, as regions.

R = geom.text (STR) returns the shapes of the letters
of the character vector STR, in UTF-8, as a 1-by-N cell array
of geom.Region objects, one for each separate piece of ink, largest
first, or the empty cell (1, 0) when there is none. A letter with a
counter, such as o or A, is a region with a hole; one in
pieces, such as i, is several regions. The outlines are the
font's own curves, straight lines and splines, so the text is as smooth at
any size. A newline in STR starts a new line. This is OpenSCAD's
text, ready to be extruded, engraved or embossed.

By default the text stands on the x axis of the world xy
plane, starting at the origin, its capitals 10 millimetres high.

Name/Value pairs:

'Height'
The height of a capital letter in millimetres, as text is sized on a
technical drawing, 10 by default. Lower case, ascenders and descenders
follow the font.
'Font'
The name of an installed font family, such as 'DejaVu Sans';
'sans-serif', 'serif' and 'monospace' name the
system's own. The default is 'sans-serif'. A font that is not
installed is an error.
'Style'
'regular', the default, 'bold', 'italic' or
'bold italic'.
'HAlign'
Which part of the text lies on the origin across: 'left', the
default, 'center' or 'right', by the box round the ink.
'VAlign'
Which part lies on the origin upwards: 'baseline', the default,
the line the letters stand on, or 'bottom', 'center' or
'top' of the box round the ink.
'Box'
The largest size [W, H] in millimetres the ink may
take, descenders included. The text is scaled evenly until it touches a
side; a size of 0 leaves that direction free, so [40, 0] makes the
